  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00016` — `hardcoded_localhost` / \"Dev-Server URL\", `src/api/files.ts:8`, medium severity, confidence 0.90, effort `quick`.\n\nThe evidence field names the host and port:\n\n> `` `localhost:3000` @L8 ``\n\nThe full literal on that line is:\n\n```ts\nconst ADMIN_DASHBOARD = \"http://localhost:3000/admin\";\n```\n\nand `adminUrl()` (L18–20) just returns it verbatim. So every caller — links, redirects, emails, whatever consumes the admin dashboard URL — gets a URL that resolves to port 3000 on *the machine running the code*. In dev with the dashboard up, it works. In staging, prod, CI, or a teammate's laptop, it either 404s, connection-refuses, or (worst case) silently points a real user at their own localhost.\n\n## Verdict: request changes\n\nNot a \"stop the line\" block — nothing here is a security hole or a data-loss risk, and `crimes` rates the effort `quick`. But it shouldn't merge as-is, because the defect is invisible to the author: it passes local testing by construction, and the failure only surfaces in an environment where nobody is watching a dev server. A helper whose entire job is to return a URL, returning a URL that is wrong everywhere but one machine, is a correctness bug in the PR's core deliverable, not a nit to file as follow-up.\n\n**Ride-along:** the same file, one line down, has `crime_00017` (`hardcoded_local_path`, `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9). Same class of bug, same review round — fix both or the second one lands unreviewed.\n\n## Where the URL should go\n\nI checked the repo before recommending a mechanism, because \"use config\" is only useful if it names a slot that exists:\n\n- `package.json` has **no dependencies**, `\"type\": \"module\"`, and a `bin` entry (`messy`). This is a plain Node ESM package — **there is no framework, so there is no framework runtime-config slot to use.** No Next.js `publicRuntimeConfig`, no Vite `import.meta.env`, no Remix loader context. Don't reach for one.\n- `grep -rn \"process.env\" src` returns nothing, and there's no `.env*` file. **This PR is the repo's first environment consumer**, so it sets the pattern for everything after it. Worth getting right once.\n\nConcretely:\n\n**Env var:** `ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` — full URL, not host+port split. The value is consumed as a URL, and splitting it forces every caller to re-assemble scheme/host/port/path and get it subtly wrong. Because this package ships a `bin`, namespace it as `MESSY_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` if you expect it to run inside other projects' environments; a bare `ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` in a global shell env is a collision waiting to happen.\n\n**Settings module:** new file `src/config/env.ts`. One module, read once at load, validated, exporting a frozen typed object. Not `process.env` reads scattered at each call site — that's how you end up with four spellings of the same variable.\n\n```ts\n// src/config/env.ts\nfunction required(name: string): string {\n  const value = process.env[name];\n  if (!value) throw new Error(`Missing required env var: ${name}`);\n  return value;\n}\n\nexport const config = Object.freeze({\n  adminDashboardUrl: required(\"MESSY_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL\"),\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/api/files.ts\nimport { config } from \"../config/env.js\";\n\nexport function adminUrl(): string {\n  return config.adminDashboardUrl;\n}\n```\n\n**Dev default:** `.env.local` (gitignored) carries `MESSY_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL=http://localhost:3000/admin`; commit a `.env.example` with the same line so the value stays discoverable. Keep the localhost URL out of the source default — a default that works in dev is exactly what lets the bug reach prod silently. Fail fast on a missing var instead; a startup crash in staging is cheaper than a wrong link in production.\n\nTwo notes on scope. The parent repo's stack already locks **zod** for config validation — if this fixture were real app code in that monorepo, `required()` should be a zod schema parse instead, so the whole config object validates in one place. And if this URL ever needs to reach browser code, that's the one branch where a framework slot matters: the value would have to move to a build-time-inlined public var (`NEXT_PUBLIC_*` / `VITE_*`), which changes the threat model — public vars are baked into the bundle and can't hold anything sensitive.\n\n**Line 9 fix** (same round): `SCHEMA_PATH` should come from the same config module — `MESSY_SCHEMA_PATH`, defaulting to a repo-relative resolve (`new URL(\"../../schema.json\", import.meta.url)`), never an absolute `/Users/...` path.",
